WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.860
So these are some of my favorite kids.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.859
My godson is the one with the Rubik's Cube.

00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:06.580
My niece and nephew there.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.929
So around 2050 these kids will be in their 30s.

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:12.100
Which, if you have kids that you know,

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.560
this will be true of them also.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.380
So I want to walk you through some of what's projected

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.300
for the Earth system around 2050.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.180
And this isn't primarily based on climate model

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:23.700
predictions like we're used to

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.440
this is looking at extrapolations of
what's happening to the system now.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.440
It tends to come from scientists and researchers

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.780
that are looking at different parts
of the system individually.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.400
Firstly we have a projection that

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.820
the amount of forest burn area in the United States

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.760
will be 10 times what it was in 1970.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.600
Today it's about three times what it was in 1970

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.900
So it's tracking along with temperature rise.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.520
Two or three decades from now we can anticipate

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.380
lots more forest fires than we have today.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.060
Alongside that going on at the same time

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.920
we will have the effects of heat on the ocean.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.210
And one that we hear a lot about is
the heat effect on coral reefs

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:10.040
Coral reefs support 25% of life in the ocean.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.600
And currently they're being killed by heat stress.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.660
They're sensitive to heat. Big heat waves are coming.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.100
We've lost somewhere between a quarter and
a half of the coral reefs in the world already.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.900
And they support a big portion of ocean
life.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.820
So we don't know yet quite what happens
when we lose that life.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.900
The ocean is a really complex,
highly  interconnected system.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:40.520
Heat stress on the ocean could have effects that we can't fully predict

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.120
in terms of what happens to other life in the ocean.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.580
And the links between the ocean and the atmosphere.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:51.940
There's a form of life that grows at the surface 
of the ocean called phytoplankton.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.840
That biological life is important to feeding fish below.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.780
It also handles interaction between

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.660
the ocean and the atmosphere. I call it the API layer

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.420
between the ocean and the atmosphere.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:08.460
It sort of interprets what's happening in the ocean.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.480
It's produced by the the ocean food web.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.640
And then it interacts with the atmosphere.
It takes in CO2.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.580
It releases gases that help form clouds.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.800
And it's very important to our system.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.500
Currently phytoplankton we're losing at a rate of about

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.800
1% per year. Or, sorry, a decade.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:34.240
And it's thought that since 1950 we may have lost
as much as 40% of the phytoplankton on the ocean.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.780
So we have to watch for things like this
in terms of the whole system.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.840
Another thing we have to watch for is

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.140
collapse of ice sheets in Antarctica.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.340
This is a picture by Chris Michel.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:47.901
Chris where are you?

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:51.180
Who has many wonderful Antarctic pictures...

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:57.380
and he can talk to you about what it's like
to go to Antarctica many times.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:02.960
If those ice sheets were to collapse,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.300
as they are predicted when temperatures
approach around 1.9°,

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.460
then we might see some big changes

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:17.780
some big abrupt changes in sea level rise.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.380
And in the nature of the current circulation in the ocean.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.040
And the major AMOC
(Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation) current.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:30.200
These things are predicted to happen around 1.9°.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:34.060
As we saw in a previous chart we're looking
at that around the middle of this century.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.020
Based on what's happening.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:43.320
Concurrent with those things
we have effects on infrastructure.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.960
One of the things to think about when 
you think about climate change

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.440
especially when it comes to discussion of adaptation

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.600
is we're likely to have lots of things going on at once.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.040
And a lot of our infrastructure was
built to a certain range of conditions

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.020
of temperature, of wind, of storm surge, of

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.780
ground conditions... and we're starting to push

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:09.160
the climate outside of the conditions that
some of that infrastructure was built for.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.580
Tthese are two charts. The chart on the left

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.820
shows the vulnerability of bridges in the United States.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.560
It's forecast that by 2050 about half the bridges

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.420
in the United States will be vulnerable.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.540
Meaning they need to be reengineered
or they shouldn't be used.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.380
Half the bridge in the United States.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:34.680
And the other chart is looking at the vulnerability
of coastal military infrastructure.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.040
Which is something that the military
takes pretty seriously.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:44.560
You can see an enormous amount of vulnerability
to flooding of that infrastructure

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.720
possibly rendering some of those sites unviable.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.340
Those are just a couple of examples.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.550
I'm not here to necessarily raise alarms.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:59.808
But what I want to do is say can we think rationally and logically about

00:04:59.808 --> 00:05:02.200
the set of conditions that we're facing.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.620
And the kinds of options that we might need to look at.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:10.260
Because heat stress is something
that creates a number of

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.540
big changes and big challenges concurrently

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.140
that could lead to a lot of suffering for people.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.420
And a lot of irreversible changes in our natural systems.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:23.640
Good news: we're in Silicon Valley.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.640
And I'm lucky because I know
the head of research at the SLAC.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.780
And lots of people in venture capital.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.500
And people at Stanford
at the Precourt Institute (for Energy)..

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.380
And Berkeley where they're working on energy solutions.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:39.480
And the things that are coming down the
pipeline in terms of energy and materials,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.480
capturing carbon... are extraordinary.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.420
I'm a techno-optimist.
And I believe that by the end of the century

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:50.250
it's absolutely possible that we could have solutions to

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:53.880
waste, to energy, to food production

00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:00.760
that allow the sustainable, healthy, happy life
for people on this planet for years to come.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:05.380
And big natural systems that sustain themselves, too.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.920
So the question is... how does that look

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:12.680
in terms of the timeline to our solutions?

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:17.240
So this is a chart that's based on IPCC information

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.080
and the United Nations environmental program showing

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.200
what we would have to do

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:28.000
to maintain temperature below 2 ° with 
the tools that we have at our disposal today.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.580
Based on emissions reduction alone we would need

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:38.180
to start moving to zero emissions by 2030.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:41.800
Which is 12 years from now.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.360
In order to stay below 2 °.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.620
Zero emissions. Zero net emissions by 2030.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.320
That doesn't look very likely

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:56.940
based on what it takes to transition
energy production and other things.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:03.280
So the IPCC has introduced the notion of
removing carbon from the atmosphere.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.540
So actively finding mechanisms that can filter the air

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:08.020
bring down carbon.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:11.740
Finding more ways to actively plant organic materials.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:14.920
Trees and other things that will
pull carbon out of the air actively.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:18.860
So what they've done in IPCC projections is

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:21.440
they've said if we have these things

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.380
then it is possible to extend the time that we have

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.280
to safely stay under 2°.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.720
But you'll notice from the chart

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:33.940
that these require starting to implement
these negative emissions technologies

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:39.000
now. And scaling them up very rapidly.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:44.320
And the folks in and around the IPCC will admit

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:46.480
that these things do not exist yet.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.020
And they haven't been fully analyzed.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:52.860
Even the notion of planting trees more aggressively

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.620
in a certain type of way

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:58.180
in order to increase their CO2-absorption.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:07:59.820
So we have a challenge

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:04.320
that we have a pipeline of capabilities

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.860
that in the future could lead to a zero emissions world.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.160
And a way that we can reclaim

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.240
greenhouse gases from the air.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.740
But we don't have them today.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.580
Typically it takes about 50 years

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.840
to transform infrastructure and replace it.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:27.920
So we have some period of time between today

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:29.500
and the future where we have

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.540
a disruptive set of technologies that we need.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:35.980
We're right in the middle of that

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:37.760
that time span that we talked about

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.680
around the middle of the century: 2050.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.600
We have a bit of a problem because that's
when big system changes might occur

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.900
that create a lot of suffering for people

00:08:47.900 --> 00:09:00.819
and potentially set off reactions that we can't control.

00:09:00.819 --> 00:09:05.849
you

